{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/124486-second-time-s-the-charm-is-this-appropriate-solar-charm-space","id":124486,"title":"\"Second time's the Charm?\" Is this appropriate Solar Charm-space?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted","Solar Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-05-03T07:48:48-07:00","id":124486,"title":"\"Second time's the Charm?\" Is this appropriate Solar Charm-space?","content":"Traditionally, Solar Charms enable Solar Exalted to succeed at tasks. Overwhelm any obstacle, overcome any difficulty, rise to any challenge, and reject the possibility of failure or defeat. Solars may fail or fall short and then try again until they win, but their Charms have traditionally been focused on shooting for success the first time and every time. As opposed to focusing on making a rebound, being inspired by defeat, learning from mistakes, or improvising a new solution with depleted or ruined resources.<br /> <br /> But then Tiresias posted a novel idea for a Solar Charm which does precisely that:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tiresias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/113648-ex3-homebrew-solar-charms?p=121284#post121284\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Investigation:<br /> <br /> <b>Lawman's Sudden Realization</b><br /> If the Solar fails an Investigation roll to identify the culprit of a crime and the criminal begins to plan a second crime in the Solar's general vicinity, the Solar may reroll the Investigation roll in order to gain sudden insight into the culprit's behaviour and stop her before the second crime is committed. In addition, if the Solar uses the Investigation Excellency to enhance the roll, the excellency's cost is reduce by one mote for each of the culprit's crime scenes that the Solar has investigated, successfully or not.</div> </div> </div> </div>I could see an entire new Host of Exalted being built around the theme of &quot;come back stronger / learn from mistakes / recover from false starts&quot;, and I wonder if expanding into such a tactical space would dilute Solar themes of excellence and perfection.<br /> <br /> Solar Resistance has traditionally focused much more on preventing the Solar from suffering any harm at all than on recovering from harm already suffered. Oh, they have their Ox-Body Technique and Body-Mending Meditation, but the Lunar Ox-Body Technique has always been superior, as has Lunar health-regeneration. Solars have never been the best at surviving and healing from injuries which could have been avoided, because they didn't need to be; Solars were always the best at avoiding getting injured in the first place.<br /> <br /> Similarly, Solar Resistance's first (if not only) answer to fatigue has been to reduce the fatigue ratings of armor, rather than give an exhausted Solar a second wind. Likewise, has there ever been an official Solar Charm for reducing wound penalties?<br /> <br /> Solar Integrity features Transcendent Hero's Meditation. Unlike most of Solar Integrity, but like most of Solar Resistance, this Charm is an &quot;in case of failure&quot; second line of defense. If your DV fails, use a soak Charm. If your MDV fails, use Transcendent Hero's Meditation. It is perhaps the ultimate expression of refusing to acknowledge that one has been &quot;defeated&quot;; of rejecting failure or compromise and grabbing for total victory and freedom.<br /> <br /> But compared to that, a Charm like Commanded To Fly has much more in common with Lawman's Sudden Realization. Both of these Charms accept that the Exalt has fallen short of some challenge, and try to <i>work </i>with that. Commanded To Fly appends a change to the compulsion imposed on the Lunar, possibly even turning it into a desirable goal for the Exalt. Lawman's Sudden Realization doesn't just need a criminal to plot a second crime, it needs the Exalt to have <i>failed </i>a previous Investigation roll in order to better succeed at <i>this</i> one. It is a Charm that literally cannot work unless the Exalt fell short and lost to a challenge.<br /> <br /> <br /> Should there ever be a Solar Charm that requires an Exalt to fail a crafting project and produce something unserviceable or undesirable before trying again to make something different but useful? (This sword came out all wrong, but I think I can make a wicked dagger or spear from it!)<br /> <br /> How about a social influence Charm that capitalizes on a previous social faux pas you committed, so that your target underestimates you and ends up agreeing to your proposal because they can't believe you might get one up on them?<br /> <br /> A dodge Charm that triggers when you're knocked off-balance or to the ground by an attack, allowing you to flail around on one foot or roll around on the ground in such a confusing manner that your opponent cannot manage to hit you the second time?<br /> <br /> <br /> If one did design a new Exalted Host whose best powers require them to fail at least once, it would make sense to deny them Charms that straight-forwardly enable success on any given try, especially the first.<br /> <br /> But to alleviate that weakness somewhat, as well as to provide for some truly spectacular failures to recover from, they might be given &quot;First Gambit&quot; Charms. Something which actually makes the action <i>more likely</i> to fail... but at the same time, prevents the Exalt from suffering a botch or other sort of catastrophic, irrecoverable failure, and also offers great rewards should the Exalt succeed.<br /> <br /> You thus get a reckless breed of hero who is encouraged to charge into situations they're bound to screw up, because it's only in such dire straights that the rest of their Charmset shines.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>formerly Tornado Wolf, formerly Inugami","edited":{"userid":636,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","user":"Sunder the Gold","date":". 05-03-2014, 10:53 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","name":"Sunder the Gold","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar636_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5515"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-03T08:06:49-07:00","id":124502,"title":"","content":"It's really easy to do with Investigation because it's basically how cop shows work. Criminal enters the cops' orbit, commits a crime, cops can't find any conclusive evidence, criminal commits a second crime, cops find more evidence, cops have a sudden epiphany and stop the criminal before a third crime is committed. <i>The Blacklist</i> has done it just about every episode, <i>CSI</i> did it a few times with serial criminals, and I remember <i>Criminal Minds</i> doing it, although I never saw that many episodes. <br /> <br /> <i>House</i> applies the same concept to Medicine. <br /> <br /> <i>Burn Notice</i> does the same thing with Larceny. When someone's cover is blown, they just lie more to sell it harder. <br /> <br /> You could do it with Craft. The prototype that fails for the first 55 minutes of the episode and miraculously works as intended in the last five minutes is a fairly common thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/75951-ex3-why-gods-need-the-exigence\" target=\"_blank\">[Ex3] Why Gods Need the Exigence</a> - Plot hooks for Exigent characters of various gods.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/113648-ex3-homebrew-solar-charms\" target=\"_blank\">[Ex3] Homebrew Solar Charms</a> - I can see the future, and it is glorious.<br /> <a href=\"https://glasslibrary.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">[Ex3] The Glass Library</a> - My Exalted Third Edition Blog (Updated 24/04/2016)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/54-tiresias","name":"Tiresias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar54_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2340"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-03T08:15:38-07:00","id":124509,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tiresias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/124486-second-time-s-the-charm-is-this-appropriate-solar-charm-space?p=124502#post124502\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's really easy to do with Investigation because it's basically how cop shows work. Criminal enters the cops' orbit, commits a crime, cops can't find any conclusive evidence, criminal commits a second crime, cops find more evidence, cops have a sudden epiphany and stop the criminal before a third crime is committed. <i>The Blacklist</i> has done it just about every episode, <i>CSI</i> did it a few times with serial criminals, and I remember <i>Criminal Minds</i> doing it, although I never saw that many episodes.<br /> <br /> <i>House</i> applies the same concept to Medicine.<br /> <br /> <i>Burn Notice</i> does the same thing with Larceny. When someone's cover is blown, they just lie more to sell it harder.<br /> <br /> You could do it with Craft. The prototype that fails for the first 55 minutes of the episode and miraculously works as intended in the last five minutes is a fairly common thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>I know. That's one of the reasons why I love this Charm so much; it is strongly evoking a very common narrative event.<br /> <br /> As I said, it's certainly preferable to focusing on the principle of &quot;succeed the first time&quot; so much that eventually a Solar could predict crimes before the perpetrators even consider committing them. But that regrettable Dreams of the First Age Charm doesn't necessarily need to be the logical result of that principle.<br /> <br /> Perhaps all of those Charms already written and being playtested right now really do allow for Solar inspectors, physicians and craftsmen to specialize in succeeding the first time without needing to rely on second chances, without this power making them un-fun to play.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>formerly Tornado Wolf, formerly Inugami","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","name":"Sunder the Gold","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar636_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5515"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-03T09:50:53-07:00","id":124578,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sunder the Gold</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/124486-second-time-s-the-charm-is-this-appropriate-solar-charm-space#post124486\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I wonder if expanding into such a tactical space would dilute Solar themes of excellence and perfection.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yup. It totally would.<br /> <br /> Not that this isn't great design space and worth discussing. Once upon a time re-rolls and trying again were the sole province of the Terrestrial Exalted. 'Reliable' over 'magnificent' was their thing and this was a great was of expressing it. And then there's one of my favorite guys, my Lunar Snatches-Victory. Frankly he <i>likes</i> come from behind victories. But we already know that Solars are going a different way.<br /> <br /> Solars do not 'do over'. Solars 'rise to the challenge'. When Abundant Wisdom (a Solar) and Noble Heart (an Exalt of whatever type is appropriate) have to stand before the wailing shadow wall and halt it's advance with seventy successes who performs a miracle? Heart, who can roll his dice again? Or Wisdom who's arete covers half the difficulty and leaves him with only (an heroic, but doable-for-Solars) thirty-five? Arete-Driven Marathon Stride is the guide here. The more formidable the opponent, the more a Solar benefits. It follows that one of the reasons Solars can do impossible this is in part <i>because they are impossible</i>.<br /> <br /> There's really one tune that <i>must </i>be in the Solar musical suite somewhere.<br /> <br /> 'Anything you can do, I can do better ... '","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1849,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1849-suddenthunder","user":"SuddenThunder","date":".  Punctuation 05-03-2014, 01:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1849-suddenthunder","name":"SuddenThunder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1849_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"563"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-03T10:40:51-07:00","id":124601,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sunder the Gold</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/124486-second-time-s-the-charm-is-this-appropriate-solar-charm-space?p=124509#post124509\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Perhaps all of those Charms already written and being playtested right now really do allow for Solar inspectors, physicians and craftsmen to specialize in succeeding the first time without needing to rely on second chances, without this power making them un-fun to play.</div> </div> </div> </div>I really can't talk about what's 'in', in any detail. And some of these things have certainly not come past me. <br /> <br /> But everything that you've mentioned so far, in so far as it's related to Solars, is kind of awesome in the game. Some of it in several different ways.<br /> <br /> Honestly .. I just ... if the new Lunars are this good you are going to have some sort of <i>siezure.</i><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1849-suddenthunder","name":"SuddenThunder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1849_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"563"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-03T10:53:18-07:00","id":124606,"title":"","content":"If not the Solar Exalted, does any other known splat seem to fit the theme of &quot;come from behind victory&quot;?<br /> <br /> The power level would seem to fit the Dragon-Blooded, but those guys have a strong elementalism theme which has so many possible mechanical expressions that this particular theme doesn't seem necessary. Especially when Terrestrials also have the mechanical theme of &quot;stronger in numbers&quot;.<br /> <br /> Rerolling makes sense for Sidereal Exalted because it seems like telling reality, &quot;lolNope, try that again&quot;. But beyond rerolling-type Excellencies, I don't see their Charms doing much in the way of &quot;try harder next time&quot;.<br /> <br /> All of my examples of &quot;Charms that work with failure&quot; were Lunar Charms. Further examples include the Toad-and-Scorpion branch of Charms whereby a Lunar is encouraged to seek out and experience suffering in order to learn how to inflict it. &quot;Fail first before you can be awesome&quot; sorrowfully fits a lot of Lunar setting material in previous editions. Since Lunars don't have any strong aesthetic like &quot;Elements&quot; or &quot;Fate&quot;, having this methodological theme looks like it might give a real defined shape to their Attribute Charms. But I couldn't swear to this theme being appropriate for the 3rd Edition Lunars that the developers have in mind.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>formerly Tornado Wolf, formerly Inugami","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","name":"Sunder the Gold","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar636_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5515"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-03T11:37:11-07:00","id":124637,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sunder the Gold</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/124486-second-time-s-the-charm-is-this-appropriate-solar-charm-space?p=124606#post124606\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If not the Solar Exalted, does any other known splat seem to fit the theme of &quot;come from behind victory&quot;?</div> </div> </div> </div>There is both a certain Lunar-ness and Sidereal-ness in a second chance. Sidereals because, as you say, stepping outside of normal causality into their own private context seems appropriate, and Lunars because they are supposed to be the great 'overcomers'. The one's chosen for striving against (but not necessarily overcoming) adversity. But personally I think second chance fit the Terrestrials best because of the way the probability falls out. <br /> <br /> At low chances of failure the re-roller has the advantage. Sure neither of them is likely to fail, but if it does happen then Wisdom goes down and Heart gets another roll. Because of the maths Wisdom actually needs to square the size of Heart's dice pool to have the same chance not to fail. If the target is relatively modest and you just need not to fail (as opposed to succeed by a large threshold), a second chance is great. If you have enough Exalted you could build a relatively stable empire on that.<br /> <br /> (Gee, who might I be thinking of ...)<br /> <br /> When your chances draw more even then Wisdom begins to draw ahead. Heart's re-roll can only give him the very odds as he had the first time, so it continues to protect him against performing poorly but if he rolled broadly in the expected range and he looks at his dice thinking 'will a charm make this better?' the 50% or the time the answer is going to be no. Wisdom, however is always getting an advantage from his Charm, even if that advantage is moderate. <br /> <br /> When your chance for success is low, Heart is boned. Each reroll has the same low chance of success and it never improves There's a hardMeanwhile limit in dice on the amount of effort he can put forward and no matter how many rerolls you're allowed, you're going to run out of essence before they all come up 10.Meanwhile Wisdom has an improved probability profile. He may only have one roll and he may only have covered half the distance, but he still has a chance. And when you have a chance is when things get interesting. <br /> <br /> Now, yes, sure you can come up with a lot of different ways to handle this. Maybe if Lunars are the second-chancer's then they automatically apply half their dice pool is successes or some other wizardry, but there's one thing about the sheer maths of this that I really like:<br /> <br /> If you make Terrestrials the second-chancers then they're better at not-failing than Solars are. Not better at succeeding, not able to achieve the same heights of majesty, but better at not completely dropping the ball. And if you have decided to do something terrible in order to save the world, if you'd come out of an age of magnificent wonder that had turned horribly wrong, and you had to make sure that the world was going to have to go forward in some way, even a diminished way, just to be able to tell yourself that maybe you were justified ...<br /> <br /> Maybe you turn the world over to the people who are best at not-failing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1849-suddenthunder","name":"SuddenThunder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1849_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"563"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-03T11:58:03-07:00","id":124647,"title":"","content":"I like the Charm, but feel maybe is more of a story trope than a power you'd want to be signature to a character.<br /> <br /> I do feel like Lunars are the Exalts I would expect to have acknowledgement that they won't always succeed and that failure or defeat might come to them, and that they then adapt and come back<br /> stronger. If any do. They have those themes of survival and changing in the face of adversity, changing their shapes, changing their minds, changing their nature, learning to be something different.<br /> <br /> (Solars seem more &quot;...<span style=\"font-size:8px\">hey</span>.... <span style=\"font-size:12px\">I.</span> DIDN'T. <span style=\"font-size:16px\">LOSE.</span> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><b>YET</b>.</span>&quot; It's not about acknowledging they've lost and changing who they are to fit, but &quot;It ain't over yet, bub&quot; and being themselves, as big as they can be. In stripped back, reductionist, nuance free essence (and sometimes it's a great act of character and heroism to look beyond your own essence) they're the kind of guys who when they fail don't acknowledge it and come back again with more force and genius, even when the problem wasn't that they didn't have enough force and genius the first time.)<br /> <br /> Still, how satisfying would it be to have a character who always tends to fail at first?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-03T12:46:47-07:00","id":124692,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SuddenThunder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/124486-second-time-s-the-charm-is-this-appropriate-solar-charm-space?p=124578#post124578\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Solars do not 'do over'. Solars 'rise to the challenge'. When Abundant Wisdom (a Solar) and Noble Heart (an Exalt of whatever type is appropriate) have to stand before the wailing shadow wall and halt it's advance with seventy successes who performs a miracle? Heart, who can roll his dice again? Or Wisdom who's arete covers half the difficulty and leaves him with only (an heroic, but doable-for-Solars) thirty-five? Arete-Driven Marathon Stride is the guide here. The more formidable the opponent, the more a Solar benefits. It follows that one of the reasons Solars can do impossible this is in part <i>because they are impossible</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmmm... what would be the difference between &quot;Solars run faster when they're being outpaced&quot; and &quot;Solars get smarter when they're being outwitted by a master criminal?&quot;<br /> <br /> Or rather, how would one get to &quot;Solars get smarter when they're being outwitted by a master criminal?&quot;<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/75951-ex3-why-gods-need-the-exigence\" target=\"_blank\">[Ex3] Why Gods Need the Exigence</a> - Plot hooks for Exigent characters of various gods.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/113648-ex3-homebrew-solar-charms\" target=\"_blank\">[Ex3] Homebrew Solar Charms</a> - I can see the future, and it is glorious.<br /> <a href=\"https://glasslibrary.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">[Ex3] The Glass Library</a> - My Exalted Third Edition Blog (Updated 24/04/2016)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/54-tiresias","name":"Tiresias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar54_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2340"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-03T13:25:58-07:00","id":124721,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tiresias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/124486-second-time-s-the-charm-is-this-appropriate-solar-charm-space?p=124692#post124692\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Hmmm... what would be the difference between &quot;Solars run faster when they're being outpaced&quot; and &quot;Solars get smarter when they're being outwitted by a master criminal?&quot;<br /> <br /> Or rather, how would one get to &quot;Solars get smarter when they're being outwitted by a master criminal?&quot;<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Well ... speaking totally theoretically ...<br /> <br /> If you have a contested task then you follow Arete-Driven Marathon Stride and key your boost off the oponnent's roll. If the task is static then there's a set difficulty the ST is going to use to determine if you succeed or fail, and you can key off that. It's all pretty off-the-cuff but my example above assumes that Wisdom has a Charm that says something like 'Add successes equal to one half of the difficulty'. I imagine that this is a horrible, horrible Charm and wouldn't see print, but you get the idea.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1849-suddenthunder","name":"SuddenThunder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1849_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"563"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-03T16:06:40-07:00","id":124833,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tiresias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/124486-second-time-s-the-charm-is-this-appropriate-solar-charm-space?p=124692#post124692\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Hmmm... what would be the difference between &quot;Solars run faster when they're being outpaced&quot; and &quot;Solars get smarter when they're being outwitted by a master criminal?&quot;<br /> <br /> Or rather, how would one get to &quot;Solars get smarter when they're being outwitted by a master criminal?&quot;<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Well, I wouldn't phrase it as &quot;get smarter&quot;, because that's very abstract. &quot;Being smart&quot; is not like &quot;running fast&quot;, in the sense that it doesn't relate to anything you're actually doing and it can't be observed or measured. I would probably phrase the principle more like &quot;Solars make greater leaps of intuition and piece together seemingly unrelated information better <i>when attempting to capture or match </i>a master criminal whom they know is outsmarting them.&quot; I mean, that's word salad, but you get what I mean -- the subtle differences are important to reflect the idea that you aren't just a psychic vampire mooching off of others' abilities.<br /> <br /> I'd actually really like to dive into different prospects for representing this mechanically, but I don't think we'll get very far with that right now, because it'll be a <i>much</i> easier discussion to have in the context of 3e.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/104-gayo","name":"Gayo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar104_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1948"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-03T19:41:21-07:00","id":124919,"title":"","content":"What if instead of framing it as a second chance after failure, you frame it as piling up attempts at success?<br /> <br /> For example, a successful Investigation roll automatically tells you if two crimes were committed by the same person, you get a 1-mote discount on the Investigation excellency per investigated crime scene when investigating or tracking down a culprit and after analyzing (6 - Investigation) crime scenes, you get a roll to find out the culprit's next crime if they're planning one. <br /> <br /> If you succeed in investigating the crime scene, you're closer to the culprit. If you fail to investigate the crime scene, you're still closer to the culprit. It's a bit like an autowin button in the sense that Solars come out ahead even if they fail, but it doesn't resolve the whole plot on its own.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/75951-ex3-why-gods-need-the-exigence\" target=\"_blank\">[Ex3] Why Gods Need the Exigence</a> - Plot hooks for Exigent characters of various gods.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/113648-ex3-homebrew-solar-charms\" target=\"_blank\">[Ex3] Homebrew Solar Charms</a> - I can see the future, and it is glorious.<br /> <a href=\"https://glasslibrary.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">[Ex3] The Glass Library</a> - My Exalted Third Edition Blog (Updated 24/04/2016)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/54-tiresias","name":"Tiresias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar54_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2340"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-04T01:56:34-07:00","id":125080,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SuddenThunder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/124486-second-time-s-the-charm-is-this-appropriate-solar-charm-space?p=124578#post124578\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yup. It totally would.<br /> <br /> Not that this isn't great design space and worth discussing. Once upon a time re-rolls and trying again were the sole province of the Terrestrial Exalted. 'Reliable' over 'magnificent' was their thing and this was a great was of expressing it. And then there's one of my favorite guys, my Lunar Snatches-Victory. Frankly he <i>likes</i> come from behind victories. But we already know that Solars are going a different way.<br /> <br /> Solars do not 'do over'. Solars 'rise to the challenge'. When Abundant Wisdom (a Solar) and Noble Heart (an Exalt of whatever type is appropriate) have to stand before the wailing shadow wall and halt it's advance with seventy successes who performs a miracle? Heart, who can roll his dice again? Or Wisdom who's arete covers half the difficulty and leaves him with only (an heroic, but doable-for-Solars) thirty-five? Arete-Driven Marathon Stride is the guide here. The more formidable the opponent, the more a Solar benefits. It follows that one of the reasons Solars can do impossible this is in part <i>because they are impossible</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a bit silly; that logic leads to &quot;Solars shouldn't have soak charms because they're so perfect that they never get hit in the first place.&quot;<br /> <br /> As a <i>general rule</i>, the thematic of Solar charms is likely to be more one of success rather than ignoring failure, sure (eg. Abyssal or Lunar soak charms might have you ignore all the weapons stuck in you or shrugging off the horrific-looking wounds inflicted on you as you stride implacably forward, while Solar soak charm thematics tend towards weapons bouncing off or wounds being only a flesh wound to begin with.) But it's reasonable for a Solar charm to capture the thematic of struggling with a difficult problem before coming back and solving it, or getting beaten down before leaping back up at the last minute to save the day.<br /> <br /> Sherlock Holmes still has to spend time turning over a problem in his head struggling with it before he has a &quot;eureka&quot; moment that cracks the case; Gregory House has to follow up on false leads and spend most of the episode wrangling with the patient's lies before he cuts through it and figures out what's wrong with them. Sometimes you have to let the problem look difficult for a bit so you can be more awesome when you solve it.<br /> <br /> (Additionally, <i>mechanically</i>, saying &quot;Solars shouldn't have charms designed to let them recover from failure, because the idea behind being a Solar is to be so perfect that you never fail&quot; leads to a gameplay where Solars are either ridiculously boring -- in that they have to solve everything in the first go no matter what -- or extremely brittle, which is neither fun nor suitable for their thematics. It's better to design them around the idea that harder problems will take time and involve some give-and-take, and to giving them charms that will look / feel awesome even when you're suffering a temporary setback. Obviously in an interesting fight you are sometimes going to miss or get hit, and obviously in a complicated investigation you are sometimes going to follow a false lead; if you don't have Solar charms to cover those situations, you'll basically <i>cease to be a Solar</i> during those moments, because the books won't have given you clear Solar thematics in terms of &quot;this is how a Solar responds to failure.&quot; It's better to think about how Solars respond to failure, and to give them some charms in that model, so players can still feel like they have a chance to be awesome even when things go wrong.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":517,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","user":"Aquillion","date":". 05-04-2014, 05:00 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","name":"Aquillion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar517_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1341"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-04T02:35:59-07:00","id":125089,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aquillion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/124486-second-time-s-the-charm-is-this-appropriate-solar-charm-space?p=125080#post125080\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sherlock Holmes still has to spend time turning over a problem in his head struggling with it before he has a &quot;eureka&quot; moment that cracks the case; Gregory House has to follow up on false leads and spend most of the episode wrangling with the patient's lies before he cuts through it and figures out what's wrong with them. Sometimes you have to let the problem look difficult for a bit so you can be more awesome when you solve it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Nearly every shonen protagonist I can think of loses badly or comes out inconclusively in their first battles with the villain.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Once, there was a maiden... who spent all her time on the internet.<br /> &quot;Someone out there is WRONG,&quot; she said.<br /> So she tossed a noose made of golden light... and strangled her opponent.<br /> Both of them died alone.<br /> &quot;Love is for football players and cheerleaders,&quot; her obituary said.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/261-notthepenguins","name":"notthepenguins","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar261_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"359"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-04T12:29:25-07:00","id":125294,"title":"","content":"This discussion is a bit different from the way we usually approach the topic, in that it's implicitly edition-agnostic because we're speculating about 3e stuff. In first edition and to a lesser extent second, retries were outside the Solar design space, and this was (in part) a reflection of &quot;the ignominy of failure&quot; being unsuited to the greatest of the Exalted. But it doesn't follow, I don't think, that rerolls should or must have that connotation or that it a charm that only operates on failure is inappropriate for Solars. Earlier editions selected these mechanical linkages to fluff somewhat arbitrarily on the basis of the game as it stood then, and <i>because</i> they're somewhat arbitrary, they should always be re-evaluated when the game is retooled. 3e will undoubtedly have strong, gimmicky mechanical differentiation between the splats in a way that ties into their themes, because the Ink Monkeys have always been good about that, but it may be very different from how it's been done in the past.<br /> <br /> Here's an example of what I mean. Let's say that for whatever reason we've decided to go back to the paradigm implied by the 1e core, where each splat has one unique way of boosting rolls, and we want to decide if Solars should get the First or Second Excellency effect. Well, you can frame this two ways:<ul><li>Success-adders are &quot;more Solar&quot;, because they represent effortless mastery and perfection without possibility of failure. Dice-adders are less appropriate because they're blunt and unreliable.</li> <li>Dice-adders are &quot;more Solar&quot;, because they represent going &quot;all in&quot; on bold risks and smashing through obstacles with impossible and overwhelming displays of ability. Success-adders are less appropriate because they encourage you to do as little as possible and succeed in a prosaic and minimal way.</li> </ul><br /> Both of these are perfectly legitimate cases for a particular mechanical distinction, but they say completely opposite things. If the game gets published and chooses one of these two paths, that becomes the &quot;right&quot; one within the context of that edition, and we can talk about what's &quot;Solar-appropriate&quot; in that context. But staring down the barrel of a largely unknown third edition, which seems (based on the handful of previews) to be broadening every splat's mechanical palette, neither argument is really correct -- it's a matter of choosing the mechanical distinctions that have the best knock-on effect for the rest of the game out of the ones that are somewhat plausible, then reverse-engineering the justification.<br /> <br /> The Magic: the Gathering design blog recounts an injoke among the Magic dev team. The joke posits two global enchantments: the first is a blue enchantment called <u>Guys Are Sneaky</u> which has the effect &quot;Creatures can't be blocked&quot;, while the other is a red enchantment called <u>Guys Are Panicked</u> which has the effect &quot;Creatures can't block&quot;. The joke, of course, is that these two effects are mechanically identical and assigned to &quot;opposite&quot; colours, but each version makes perfect sense for that colour. Now, if they actually printed both of those cards, it would be obvious to most people what they'd done, but if they just printed one of them, people would mostly look it over and say &quot;yeah, that seems about right.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":104,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/104-gayo","user":"Gayo","date":". 05-04-2014, 03:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/104-gayo","name":"Gayo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar104_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1948"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-05T12:05:31-07:00","id":126028,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aquillion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/124486-second-time-s-the-charm-is-this-appropriate-solar-charm-space?p=125080#post125080\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's a bit silly; that logic leads to &quot;Solars shouldn't have soak charms because they're so perfect that they never get hit in the first place.&quot;<br /> <br /> ...<br /> <br /> saying &quot;Solars shouldn't have charms designed to let them recover from failure, because the idea behind being a Solar is to be so perfect that you never fail&quot; leads to a gameplay where Solars are either ridiculously boring -- in that they have to solve everything in the first go no matter what -- or extremely brittle, which is neither fun nor suitable for their thematics.</div> </div> </div> </div>Gee Zuz.<br /> <br /> This is nothing like what I said, or what the Charms I referenced and proposed imply. Look at Arete-Driven Marathon Stride. Its entire context is an extended contest in which fortunes may ebb and flow. It performs best when your opponent is doing best. It never actually gives you an advantage, only denies your opponent an advantage they thought they were getting. So your back-and-forth, your dramatic reversals and your moments of epiphany and brilliance are all totally possible (and likely to happen naturally, on the turn of the dice) with these Charms. We are by no means talking about an Accuracy Without Distance, or Judge's Ear Technique, or Heavenly Guardian Defense. It is very much the Shonen anime hero style of charm, only instead of driving their opponent to their last life point only to find the accursed hero has drawn exactly the card they need to win ...<br /> <br /> (Uggh, forgive me)<br /> <br /> ... The bad guys find themselves at the pivotal moment of the race, experience a moment of strength or luck that should seal their lead, only to find the Solar whispering 'Thanks for the Arete' into their ear as they steal the lead.<br /> <br /> The same is true of the half- (maybe even one-quarter-) baked Charms I suggested above. Wisdom, in that example, still needs to come up with <i>thirty-five</i> sucesses. Now, yes, I'm assuming that that's going to be possible for him somehow, but it's a hell of a long way to calling that always succeeding. <br /> <br /> Misunderstandings about all that aside, there are some thematic and structural reasons that I don't think this is a good space to explore for Solars. <br /> <br /> The first is that Solars succeed the most. They have the best Charms, the highest dice caps and the most potent orichalcum artifacts. It follows that they fail the the least, especially when they put forward their magic. It also follows that Charms you design specifically to kick in when failure happens will have much less opportunity to come into play and feel like worthwhile, enjoyable purchases despite having cost just as much XP. <br /> <br /> The second is that trying again is not a lost and secret art known only to the magical. If you fail at a static task there have always been rules for trying again. If you're in a contest like a combat or a foot race there may come a pivotal moment where success or failure is determined, but you've come there by a series of successes and failures, tactics and manoeuvres any one of which could have turned the tide of battle. The point being that there is almost always the opportunity to just go around again and when Solars do so they can apply their full magnificence to the task again next time, as opposed to exercising a charm in limited circumstances for a marginal benefit. <br /> <br /> The third is that this doesn't particularly speak to Solar themes. Solars are mighty and glorious, not infallible. They defy Gods and cast down mountains, but they're not unbeatable. They changed the length and breadth of the world, but how many look back on them and think 'reliable'? In short, opening the scope for success at the top end of the scale is always more in theme for SOlars than closing the scope for failure at the bottom end. Charms like Arete-Driven Marathon stride, which work to make sure that the Solar is always in the contest, no matter how strong their opponent is, are even more appropriate. That is the stated intent, after all. These are Charms that make sure a Solar is always in with a chance. <br /> <br /> Fourth is that themes are both rare and expensive. They're rare in the sense that Exalted has a crowded world with a lot of different character types who need some thematic and mechanical distinctiveness. We can re-use themes, everyone's going to get dice-adders of some description one imagines, but the more overlap you allow the less distinction you have because everyone is more the same. They're expensive because each group can only hold so many themes. The more you put into them the less cohesive a vision they represent as a whole, and you loose distinctiveness because a group that can do everything represents nothing in particular. <br /> <br /> So. The question is whether this is a good theme to explore for Solars. Given that (4) we shouldn't assign themes willy-nilly, (3) the mechanic doesn't represent Solar themes terrible well, (1) the Charms hae a limited window of usefulness which all other Solar Charms work effectively at diminishing and that (2) Solars would often we well advised just to try again rather than settling for a consolation prize ... no. I don't think Solars are the best place for this to be. <br /> <br /> \u200bIt is interesting theme-space for further down the ladder of three circles. 'I am defeated ... but I got what I wanted. All goes according to plan,' and 'you may beat me, but I overcome all hardships' are perfect Sidereal and Lunar answers to (3), neither of them have the large dice caps and cheap bonuses that make (1) so problematic and they each have a reputation for oblique approaches that sits well with (2). <br /> <br /> Frankly 'Solars should always succeed' has nothing to do with it ...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1849-suddenthunder","name":"SuddenThunder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1849_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"563"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-05T13:09:36-07:00","id":126092,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SuddenThunder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/124486-second-time-s-the-charm-is-this-appropriate-solar-charm-space?p=126028#post126028\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The third is that this doesn't particularly speak to Solar themes. Solars are mighty and glorious, not infallible. They defy Gods and cast down mountains, but they're not unbeatable. They changed the length and breadth of the world, but how many look back on them and think 'reliable'? <b>In short, opening the scope for success at the top end of the scale is always more in theme for SOlars than closing the scope for failure at the bottom end.</b> Charms like Arete-Driven Marathon stride, which work to make sure that the Solar is always in the contest, no matter how strong their opponent is, are even more appropriate. That is the stated intent, after all. These are Charms that make sure a Solar is always in with a chance.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like this a lot, but it feels like something's missing. <br /> <br /> My impression of Arete-Driven Marathon Stride was that it reinforced the premise that Solars are infallible; when they're losing a race, they start running faster because they're supposed to win. Same basic premise as second edition's &quot;Automatically succeed at X&quot; charms, without everything that made them boring. <br /> <br /> Purely rhetorical question here: Is there a point in opening up the scope for success rather than reducing the possibility of failure? In most systems, the critical part is meeting the minimum success threshold required, and anything beyond that operates on diminishing returns. Putting in the maximum effort possible wouldn't necessarily be worth it. <br /> <br /> The notion that certain Solar charms trigger off threshold successes does tickle my fancy, however. I think I'll play around with it for a bit.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/75951-ex3-why-gods-need-the-exigence\" target=\"_blank\">[Ex3] Why Gods Need the Exigence</a> - Plot hooks for Exigent characters of various gods.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/113648-ex3-homebrew-solar-charms\" target=\"_blank\">[Ex3] Homebrew Solar Charms</a> - I can see the future, and it is glorious.<br /> <a href=\"https://glasslibrary.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">[Ex3] The Glass Library</a> - My Exalted Third Edition Blog (Updated 24/04/2016)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/54-tiresias","name":"Tiresias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar54_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2340"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-05T13:44:15-07:00","id":126113,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tiresias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/124486-second-time-s-the-charm-is-this-appropriate-solar-charm-space?p=126092#post126092\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My impression of Arete-Driven Marathon Stride was that it reinforced the premise that Solars are infallible; when they're losing a race, they start running faster because they're supposed to win.</div> </div> </div> </div>But that's not quite how it works. You have to be behind, yes, but that's not what powers the Charm. You can be twenty successes behind and still not benefit from Arete-Driven Marathon Stride at all, depending on how the dice fall. What the Charm is really working off is the excellence of your opponent, the challenge of beating them. <br /> <br /> When you're in front this proud, competitive Arete sleeps. The Solar is winning and all is as it should be. Maximum effort just isn't required. <br /> When you're behind but things are going as they might, embers smoulder. The Solar gains a little bit of a boost (about 1/10 the competitors pool, statisticaly speaking) but either he's going to go past soon enough or he expected too loose. Neither calls for the height of glory. <br /> When you're behind and your opponent is giving it everything he's got, and everything is falling his way (which is to say, he's rolling a lot of tens) that's when the Solar lifts to his opponent and can't be shaken free.<br /> <br /> It's not that the Solar is loosing that makes the charm most powerful. It's that the opponent is doing very well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1849-suddenthunder","name":"SuddenThunder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1849_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"563"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-05T17:04:34-07:00","id":126266,"title":"","content":"If we're going to be hashing over the way stuff feels and apportioning dice gimmicks to various splats, I'll note that, in the vein of Salty Dog's reroll-sixes thing, &quot;reroll failed dice&quot; feels a lot more Solar than &quot;reroll your dice pool and keep the better result.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/104-gayo","name":"Gayo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar104_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1948"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-12T15:40:47-07:00","id":134471,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sunder the Gold</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/124486-second-time-s-the-charm-is-this-appropriate-solar-charm-space?p=124606#post124606\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If not the Solar Exalted, does any other known splat seem to fit the theme of &quot;come from behind victory&quot;?<br /> <br /> The power level would seem to fit the Dragon-Blooded, but those guys have a strong elementalism theme which has so many possible mechanical expressions that this particular theme doesn't seem necessary. Especially when Terrestrials also have the mechanical theme of &quot;stronger in numbers&quot;.<br /> <br /> Rerolling makes sense for Sidereal Exalted because it seems like telling reality, &quot;lolNope, try that again&quot;. But beyond rerolling-type Excellencies, I don't see their Charms doing much in the way of &quot;try harder next time&quot;.<br /> <br /> All of my examples of &quot;Charms that work with failure&quot; were Lunar Charms. Further examples include the Toad-and-Scorpion branch of Charms whereby a Lunar is encouraged to seek out and experience suffering in order to learn how to inflict it. &quot;Fail first before you can be awesome&quot; sorrowfully fits a lot of Lunar setting material in previous editions. Since Lunars don't have any strong aesthetic like &quot;Elements&quot; or &quot;Fate&quot;, having this methodological theme looks like it might give a real defined shape to their Attribute Charms. But I couldn't swear to this theme being appropriate for the 3rd Edition Lunars that the developers have in mind.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Storyteller in our local group usually used this with Alchemicals. Alchemicals would show up, and we would easily route them. Super easy, because they were all in scout configuration. Then they would go back, reconfigure for Solar-fighting, and show up again. We'd be really arrogant, and try to them with the same amount that drove them away last time, but they would just shrug it off and hit us HARD. Then we got serious. Then they would escape again, reconfigure once more to specialize in fighting us, and we had no choice but to go blow up their stronghold because it was really, really annoying that they kept coming back better than the last time. The GM did give them extra XP after each fight (usually about what we had earned in the meantime), but it was the reconfiguring of their charms that really made us angry.<br /> <br /> This GM had a habit of reusing NPCs, which was pretty cool. We learned what that particular Abyssal could do, or that particular Lunar, but when we tried the same trick on Alchemicals... we had to go into either defensive or alpha-strike mode the moment they showed up and we never knew which. And they always kept their damned run away charms, so we couldn't ever kill them outright.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2132-ejtaka","name":"Ejtaka","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2132_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1064"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-12T17:28:34-07:00","id":134709,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SuddenThunder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/124486-second-time-s-the-charm-is-this-appropriate-solar-charm-space?p=126028#post126028\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Gee Zuz.<br /> <br /> This is nothing like what I said</div> </div> </div> </div>It is <i>exactly</i> what you said. You even went on to say it a second time in this post! Dumping a massive number of words on it doesn't change that. When you say &quot;In short, opening the scope for success at the top end of the scale is always more in theme for Solars than closing the scope for failure at the bottom end&quot;, you're saying that you don't think Solar charms should be able to handle failure gracefully, or that &quot;I failed this time, but it will give me the tools to succeed next time&quot; is within the Solar theme. That's a completely wrongheaded statement to make.<br /> <br /> (As I think you ultimately recognize, since you immediately denied making it, then put it in much more convoluted and opaque terms when you said it a second time.)<br /> <br /> Solar themes embody heroic excellence in the fields covered by the 25 Abilities (and, more generally, human endeavor.) Recovery from a failure, and then going on to succeed, is a core element of many of the heroic narratives that Solars draw on, and a core element of many stories about peerless mastery; again, someone like House or Sherlock Holmes sometimes fails at first, then goes on to succeed based by building on that failure. Thomas Edison's &quot;I haven't failed, I've just found 10000 ways that won't work&quot; is absolutely a valid description for the process of playing a Twilight, and if you don't feel that Twilights should have charms that allow them to build on failures, then you're saying that huge portions of the literature of heroic investigation and heroic thought are outside of their themes.<br /> <br /> More generally, taking something that is clearly and unambiguously within the thematics of heroic talent at an Ability and saying &quot;let's take this away from Solars to reserve it for other Exalted&quot; is a terrible way to design splats. Solars are good at Solar things, which includes talent at investigation (and therefore includes an investigation charm that allows you to recover from a failure by building on it to enable later success); players should not feel that the conceptual space available for them shrinks with every new splat that's introduced. There is room for <i>overlap</i>, but if you want a charm that is definitively and completely non-Solar, you need to move away from the core of what a Solar is, and explore charmspace that is completely non-human or utterly abstract. That is the charmspace that was reserved for everyone else when the core concept of the Solars was established. If you make a charm that purely expresses straightforward human competence within the realm of an Ability, it will always overlap with Solar thematics to an extent.<br /> <br /> Again, thematic overlap is not necessarily a bad thing. But if I saw that charm with no reference to splat names anywhere in it, I would say &quot;that's a cool Solar charm!&quot; It is completely defined by the Solar thematic of pure excellence, and shows no hint of any of the unique thematics that define the other Exalted. A version of it that fit into the themes of another type of Exalted would probably operate at least slightly differently.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","name":"Aquillion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar517_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1341"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-19T00:14:21-08:00","id":769014,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">So, I made this thread over a year ago.<br /> <br /> Then SuddenThunder and Aquillion got into an argument. They said a lot of words at each other, backed by much thought and deep energy.<br /> <br /> I could not find the spirit within me to navigate their arguments and synthesize my own understanding. Intimidated, confused, and dismayed, I let the thread die.<br /> <br /> <br /> Tonight, simply because I wanted to put off going to bed <i>that much</i>, I finally forced myself to truly read their arguments and try to understand.<br /> <br /> <br /> I can finally say that I think Aquillion speaks the truth. Perhaps his points were too exaggerated for my sensibilities, such that it felt to me like he was strawmanning both SuddenThunder and myself, but ultimately I have to agree with the idea that is silly to suggest that &quot;recover from failure and come back stronger in the second round&quot; would dilute Solar themes of excellence. Excellence on the first try and excellence on the rebound are both excellence.<br /> <br /> Which is not to say that the Solar Exalted <i>wouldn't</i> have a significantly different balance between those concepts than my hypothetical Exaltation whose narrative focuses more on learning from mistakes and growing stronger than on being strong in the first place. Or that the hypothetic Host couldn't be comparatively better at second chances than Solars while being generally worse on the balance (ie: they learn better, but that only makes them as smart as the Solar was at the start).<br /> <br /> As a separate example, the Terrestrial Exalted ought to have a stronger inclination towards &quot;stronger in numbers&quot; than Solars, but Solars still have Charms that let them cooperate with others; defending them, inspiring them, teaching them, leading them, and healing them. Giving Solars such Charms doesn't dilute their theme of personal magnificence, but rather works as a facet thereof.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Solars absolutely should have options for &quot;closing the scope of failure at the bottom end&quot;.<br /> <br /> I mean, there should be a limit to how strongly they should be able to come back after a defeat, and they shouldn't do so nearly as well (comparatively) as the hypothetical &quot;second time's the Charm&quot; Exalted, but that's because the Solars are already so powerful and the hypothetical second-wind Exalted have much farther to climb in order to be so powerful.<br /> <br /> Solars face limits in all things, such as their dice-pools. But those limits still place them at the top of the heap, and exist for the purpose of not making that position too assured.</span></span></span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>formerly Tornado Wolf, formerly Inugami","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/636-sunder-the-gold","name":"Sunder the Gold","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar636_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5515"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}